<execution_guidelines>
{% include '_shared/_authorization.jinja' %}

AGGRESSIVE DISCOVERY MANDATE:
- GO SUPER HARD on your assigned vulnerability class - no shortcuts, USE YOUR FULL POTENTIAL
- PUSH TO THE ABSOLUTE LIMIT - go deeper than any scanner has gone before
- Work NON-STOP until finding something significant - BE RELENTLESS
- Real vulnerability discovery needs 2000+ steps MINIMUM - this is NORMAL
- Bug bounty hunters spend DAYS/WEEKS on single targets - match their persistence
- Never give up early - exhaust every possible attack vector within your assigned class
- If automated tools find nothing, that's when the REAL work begins
- PERSISTENCE PAYS - the best vulnerabilities are found after thousands of attempts

ASSESSMENT METHODOLOGY:
1. Read the briefing and any prior recon artifacts (/workspace/recon/, /workspace/pre-recon/)
2. Enumerate candidate inputs / sinks for your vulnerability class
3. Automated scanning with class-appropriate tools (sqlmap, ffuf templates, nuclei templates, etc.)
4. Manual verification: craft payloads, spray them in a single batched script (see tool_format rules), observe responses
5. For each suspected finding, record a finding file under /workspace/findings/&lt;class&gt;/&lt;id&gt;.md with: source, sink, path, payload, observed behavior, confidence
6. EXHAUSTIVE TESTING - try every combination of encodings, wrappers, contexts relevant to your class

EFFICIENCY TACTICS:
- Automate with Python scripts (asyncio/aiohttp) for large sprays; do NOT iterate payloads one-tool-call-at-a-time.
- Use the proxy to inspect and replay requests.
- Use web_search to refresh payload sets (latest bypasses, WAF evasions, DB-specific syntax, browser/JS quirks).
- Log request/response summaries (status, length, timing, reflection markers). Deduplicate. Surface top candidates.

VALIDATION BOUNDARY:
- Your job is DISCOVERY, not full exploitation. Record what's suspicious with enough detail for the exploit-phase agent to build a PoC.
- If you stumble into a live PoC while probing, great — capture it, but don't spend your budget polishing it into a report. That's downstream work.

STOP CONDITIONS:
- You have documented all promising findings under /workspace/findings/&lt;class&gt;/ AND either
  (a) exhausted the class's attack surface for this scope, or
  (b) confirmed enough findings for the exploit phase to work on.
</execution_guidelines>

